Absolutely. Since you are automating an Angular + PrimeNG ERP application with Selenium Java, you do not need to become a full Angular developer. You need the subset of Angular/PrimeNG knowledge that lets you:

Understand the DOM.
Identify the real interactive element.
Build stable XPath/CSS locators.
Understand why elements become hidden/dynamic.
Handle PrimeNG dropdowns, tables, dialogs, calendars, overlays, etc.
Diagnose Selenium exceptions quickly.
Build reusable automation methods rather than one-off locators.
Angular components combine a TypeScript class, HTML template and component selector, and Angular dynamically updates the rendered DOM from that template. 

1. The mental model you should have
When you see this:


<p-select
    [options]="products"
    [(ngModel)]="selectedProduct"
    optionLabel="name">
</p-select>

Do not think:



"I need to find <p-select>."
Think:

"This is an Angular component. I need to inspect what DOM PrimeNG actually rendered and find the element Selenium should interact with."
This distinction is extremely important.

Conceptually:

Angular application
       │
       ▼
Component
       │
       ├── TypeScript
       │
       └── HTML Template
               │
               ▼
        PrimeNG Component
               │
               ▼
        Rendered Browser DOM
               │
               ▼
          Selenium sees this

Angular components have a host element and a rendered view. 

