3. Angular Components
Suppose developers write:


<app-product-details></app-product-details>

That is an Angular component.

You may see:


<app-product-details _ngcontent-ng-c123>
</app-product-details>

Or:


<xbs-dcloud-dropdown-group>
    ...
</xbs-dcloud-dropdown-group>

These are component/custom-element boundaries.

Important
The outer component is not necessarily the clickable element.

For example:


<xbs-dcloud-dropdown-group>
    <p-dropdown>
        <div>
            <span role="combobox">
                Product
            </span>
        </div>
    </p-dropdown>
</xbs-dcloud-dropdown-group>

Selenium should probably interact with:


//span[@role='combobox']

rather than:


//xbs-dcloud-dropdown-group

Rule
Locate the element that performs the user action, not merely the Angular component that owns it.

4. Angular template vs browser DOM
This is one of the most important concepts for you.

Developers might write:


<p-dropdown
    [options]="products"
    [(ngModel)]="product">
</p-dropdown>

But Chrome DevTools may show something substantially different.

You might see:


<p-dropdown class="p-element">
    <div class="p-dropdown">
        <span
            role="combobox"
            aria-haspopup="listbox"
            aria-expanded="false">
        </span>
    </div>
</p-dropdown>

The HTML in the source/template and the final rendered DOM are not necessarily identical.

For Selenium
Always prioritize:

Rendered DOM
      ↓
HTML structure
      ↓
Stable attributes
      ↓
Relationships
      ↓
XPath

Not:

Angular source
      ↓
Guess XPath

5. Angular data binding
You will frequently see:


<input [value]="productName">

or:


<button [disabled]="isSaving">

or:


<div [class.active]="selected">

The square brackets mean Angular property binding. Angular uses bindings to dynamically update DOM properties and attributes. 

Automation implication
This:


<button [disabled]="isSaving">

may become:


<button disabled>

or:


<button>

depending on application state.

Therefore, Selenium should usually wait for the current state, rather than assume the element is immediately interactable.

6. Angular event binding
You may see:


<button (click)="saveProduct()">

or:


<input (keyup)="search()">

or:


<div (mouseenter)="showMenu()">

This tells you something important:

The UI is state-driven.

Clicking something may cause:

Click
  ↓
Angular event
  ↓
TypeScript method
  ↓
API call
  ↓
Angular state update
  ↓
DOM changes

Therefore:


element.click();
element2.click();

is often unreliable when done immediately.

You need synchronization:


wait.until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(locator));

or:


wait.until(ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(locator));

7. Angular @if / *ngIf
Older Angular applications commonly use:


<div *ngIf="showProduct">
    Product Details
</div>

Modern Angular also has:


@if (showProduct) {
    <div>
        Product Details
    </div>
}

This means the element may not exist in the DOM at all until the condition becomes true.

So:


driver.findElement(locator);

may fail because the element hasn't been rendered yet.

Use:


wait.until(
    ExpectedConditions.presenceOfElementLocated(locator)
);

Then, if necessary:


wait.until(
    ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(locator)
);

Angular's control-flow/template system can create and remove DOM content dynamically. 

8. Angular *ngFor / @for
This is extremely important for ERP tables.

Example:


<tr *ngFor="let product of products">
    <td>{{product.name}}</td>
</tr>

or modern Angular:


@for (product of products; track product.id) {
    <tr>
        <td>{{product.name}}</td>
    </tr>
}

The browser might end up with:


<tbody>
    <tr>
        <td>Coffee</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Wheat</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Rice</td>
    </tr>
</tbody>

Bad locator

//tbody/tr[2]

This is fragile.

Better

//tbody/tr[td[normalize-space()='Coffee']]

Or:


//tbody/tr[.//span[normalize-space()='Coffee']]

This is data-driven locating.

