14. Why role is extremely valuable
You should learn these attributes:

role
aria-label
aria-expanded
aria-haspopup
aria-selected
aria-controls
aria-labelledby

Example:


<span
    role="combobox"
    aria-haspopup="listbox"
    aria-expanded="false">

This is often better than:


//span[@class='p-dropdown-label p-inputtext']

because CSS classes may change with PrimeNG versions or application styling.

Prefer

//span[@role='combobox']

over:


//span[contains(@class,'p-dropdown-label')]

when the role is unique and stable.

15. aria-expanded is extremely useful
Before opening:


aria-expanded="false"

After opening:


aria-expanded="true"

This gives you an excellent synchronization mechanism.

For example:


WebElement dropdown = wait.until(
    ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(locator)
);

dropdown.click();

wait.until(ExpectedConditions.attributeToBe(
    dropdown,
    "aria-expanded",
    "true"
));

This is often better than simply:


Thread.sleep(2000);

16. PrimeNG overlay concept
This is one of the biggest sources of Selenium problems.

Suppose your dropdown is here:

Product Details
    ↓
Table
    ↓
Product cell
    ↓
PrimeNG dropdown

You click it.

The options might not remain inside the same <td>.

Instead, PrimeNG may render the popup in an overlay container elsewhere in the DOM.

Conceptually:

TABLE
 └── TD
      └── p-dropdown
           └── combobox


BODY
 └── overlay container
      └── listbox
           ├── option
           ├── option
           └── option

This is why this can fail:


//td[... ]//li[@role='option']

while this works:


//li[@role='option']

This is critical PrimeNG knowledge.

17. Hidden duplicate overlays
You may see:


<ul role="listbox" style="display:none">

and another:


<ul role="listbox">

Both exist in DOM.

Therefore:


//li[@role='option']

may return:

2 of 2

or more.

Even if your dropdown locator is:

1 of 1

the option locator can still have multiple matches.

That's why we discussed filtering:


WebElement visibleOption = wait.until(driver ->
    driver.findElements(option)
        .stream()
        .filter(WebElement::isDisplayed)
        .findFirst()
        .orElse(null)
);

This is a very useful PrimeNG automation pattern.

