25. Recommended dropdown method
For your actual application, I'd structure it like:


public void selectTableDropdownValue(
        String columnName,
        String value) {

    By dropdownLocator =
        getItemDetailsDropdown(columnName);

    WebElement dropdown =
        webDriverCommonLibrary
            .explicitWaitVisibility(driver, dropdownLocator);

    scrollToElement(dropdown);

    dropdown =
        webDriverCommonLibrary
            .explicitWaitVisibility(driver, dropdownLocator);

    webDriverCommonLibrary
        .explicitWaitClickable(driver, dropdownLocator)
        .click();

    By optionLocator = By.xpath(
        "//li[@role='option' and " +
        "normalize-space(@aria-label)='" +
        value.trim() +
        "']"
    );

    WebElement option = wait.until(driver ->
        driver.findElements(optionLocator)
            .stream()
            .filter(WebElement::isDisplayed)
            .findFirst()
            .orElse(null)
    );

    option.click();
}

Notice something important:

We keep the By
instead of holding a WebElement for a long time.

That's useful because Angular/PrimeNG can rerender the component.

26. Why keeping By is better in Angular
Suppose:


WebElement dropdown = driver.findElement(locator);

Then Angular updates the table.

The original element can be destroyed.

You now have:

Java WebElement
       ↓
OLD DOM ELEMENT ❌

while the browser has:

NEW DOM ELEMENT ✅

This causes:

StaleElementReferenceException

If you keep:


By locator

Selenium can find the latest element again.

Therefore:


wait.until(
    ExpectedConditions.elementToBeClickable(locator)
).click();

is often safer.

